Make HB_NULL_POOL_SIZE overridable from the compiler command line.

Upstream harfbuzz dropped the #ifndef guard around HB_NULL_POOL_SIZE, so a
-DHB_NULL_POOL_SIZE=N on the command line only produces a redefinition
warning and the header's value (640) wins. The 128-bit e2k mode of the MCST
lcc compiler (built from this repo's toolchains/target-linux) has 16-byte
pointers, which pushes hb_null_size() of pointer-bearing null objects
(AAT::kern_subtable_accelerator_data_t and friends) past 640 and trips the
"Increase HB_NULL_POOL_SIZE" static_assert in every TU instantiating
Null<>/Crap<> for them. The e2k128 build passes -DHB_NULL_POOL_SIZE=2048
(common/harfbuzz.mk); restore the historical #ifndef guard so the define
actually reaches the header. Every other target keeps the stock 640.

Drop this patch when upstream restores the guard.

--- a/src/hb-null.hh
+++ b/src/hb-null.hh
@@ -36,8 +36,9 @@
  */
 
 /* Global nul-content Null pool.  Enlarge as necessary. */
-
+#ifndef HB_NULL_POOL_SIZE
 #define HB_NULL_POOL_SIZE 640
+#endif
 
 template <typename T, typename>
 struct _hb_has_min_size : hb_false_type {};
